Subject: A constructor's layout chain is built at its first construct, and the layout breaks when `prototype` changes

What this does
  - The chain of a contracted constructor layout was built when the function object was created. For `function F(){}; F.prototype = new Base();` the assignment follows, so the chain stood on the default prototype and no instance ever reached it. It is now built from `js_create_from_ctor_cap`, the engine's single construct entry (interpreter, register VM, light constructs, `qjs_aot_construct_this`), on the first construct of a function whose bytecode still has `aot_layout_ctor` set: `F.prototype` is read after any ES5 assignment and before the construct reads it. `js_closure` no longer calls the hook; a class (`js_op_define_class`, `prototype` is not writable) still builds at definition.
  - The mark `aot_layout_ctor` is dropped after that one attempt (built or refused), so a later construct costs nothing; every function without a layout pays only the byte it never loaded before inside a branch the construct already takes.
  - New `qjs_aot_layout_watch_proto(ctx, fn, layout_id)`, called by the unit after a successful build: a fact group on `fn` listing its own `prototype` data property (the value the chain was built on). A store of a different value, a delete, a redefinition, `defineProperty`, freeze etc. reach the group through the existing lowest-level mutator events, and the group's break now calls `js_aot_layout_break` for its layout (new `JSAotFact.layout`), which clears the facts word and moves the epoch. Storing the same object again is not a change.
  - `JSAotFacts.fresh_pending` counts FRESH groups still PENDING; the construct event of `js_create_from_ctor_cap` is only raised while it is non-zero, so a watched constructor does not scan every group on each construct.

Why we need it
  Deltablue-style classes (`Scale.prototype = new Inheriter()`) kept 11 layouts in the generator but every instance shape had layout id 0: the chain was built for the replaced prototype.

Soundness
  - A layout is built only when the first instance is about to be made, so no instance of the old chain exists. After the build the identity of `F.prototype` is part of the layout's fact: any change breaks it for good (no re-pointing). Instances already made keep their shape; the broken layout sends compiled code to the generic body.
  - Shapes come from `qjs_aot_layout_build` (hash-consed `add_property` transitions of an object whose prototype is the built-on object), so they are the shapes the engine itself reaches for an instance of that prototype.
  - Only the final shape of the chain carries the layout id (leaving a layout shape breaks the layout, so intermediate shapes cannot).
  - The watched function becomes a prototype in the engine's cache sense (interpreter add/store caches stay off it), as every fact-group object does.

Functions touched
  `js_create_from_ctor_cap`, `js_closure`, `js_aot_layout_fn_hook`, `js_aot_fact_break_now`, `qjs_aot_fact_new`, `qjs_aot_fact_validate`, new `qjs_aot_layout_watch_proto`; `JSAotFact`, `JSAotFacts`; `quickjs-aot-rt.h` (declaration).

--- a/quickjs.c
